# Security

- Applicable privacy regulations are design constraints. Check `docs/adr/` for which regulations apply to this project.
- Classify personal data, store only what is necessary, define retention per category, and keep delete/export paths explicit.
- Document legal basis and purpose per data flow, and pseudonymize when full identification is unnecessary.
- Minimize PII in prompts, logs, traces, and analytics outputs.
- For AI safety: no raw data dumps to the model, structured tool outputs only, treat prompt injection as hostile input, never return secrets or out-of-scope tenant data, and do not store chain-of-thought or full chat logs as product data.
- Never log tokens or secrets, bypass permissions without an issue and documentation, use unbounded retention, or store data without a defined purpose, owner, and retention policy.
- Validate and sanitize all external input. Assume it is hostile.
- Use parameterized queries or ORM methods for all database access. Never interpolate user input into queries.
- Apply the principle of least privilege: services, users, and tokens get the minimum permissions they need.
- Check authorization at every entry point. Do not rely on the UI to enforce access control.
- Keep dependencies updated. Audit for known vulnerabilities regularly.
